PG.notes("l192", "<p class=\"summary\">Explains what a web framework is, why Node.js is a JavaScript runtime rather than a framework, what 'asynchronous and event-driven' means, and why the course picks Node for the backend.</p>\n<h3>Framework vs runtime</h3>\n<ul><li>A web framework is a set of ready-made components and structures for building an app, so common plumbing doesn't have to be hand-written every time (illustrated with store-bought vs home-made mayonnaise).</li><li>Node.js is not itself a framework. Its own docs describe it as an asynchronous, event-driven JavaScript runtime for building scalable network applications.</li><li>A runtime environment is what lets code execute somewhere. JavaScript was originally created only to run inside browsers, so on its own it could not be used for non-website programs.</li><li>Node takes Google's V8 engine (from Chromium, written in C/C++, the engine that powers Chrome) and runs it outside the browser, so JavaScript can run directly on any machine: a laptop or a server.</li><li>Result: JavaScript can now do the jobs of server-side languages such as Python, Ruby, PHP or C#, including desktop and server-side applications.</li></ul>\n<h3>Synchronous vs asynchronous, event-driven</h3>\n<ul><li>Synchronous code (all the scripts written so far) runs top to bottom; each step waits for the previous one to finish, holding resources until it does.</li><li>Analogy: ordering a parcel online and being unable to do anything until it arrives would be the synchronous version.</li><li>Asynchronous code starts a slow task (file read, network call, database query) and moves on instead of waiting. The follow-up code is packaged as a handler for an event and runs only when that event fires (the 'parcel arrives').</li><li>This non-blocking model is why Node is fast and efficient for servers that handle many requests at once.</li><li><span class=\"extra\">Extra:</span> In code this shows up as callback functions, Promises and async/await, which appear in the next lessons (e.g. fs.writeFile's callback).</li></ul>\n<h3>Where Node fits in the backend</h3>\n<ul><li>The backend is server + application + database. The server is just a computer with no browser, so something has to run the application code: Node does that for JavaScript.</li><li>Node lets you build the application part of the backend in JavaScript on that server.</li></ul>\n<h3>Why the course chooses Node</h3>\n<ul><li>One language across the whole stack: HTML/CSS/JS on the frontend and JS on the backend. Easier for frontend devs to move to backend, and code can be shared between client and server.</li><li>Scales well for large professional projects.</li><li>Fast and non-blocking thanks to the async, event-driven design.</li><li>Huge ecosystem: thousands of open-source packages on NPM, plus plenty of docs, tutorials and Stack Overflow answers.</li><li>Used in production by companies such as LinkedIn, Twitter, Netflix and NASA.</li><li>Node is a key skill employers look for. Python backends (Flask) are a good fit for ML/AI work; PHP and Ruby on Rails are presented as outdated choices for a new bootcamp curriculum. GitHub language stats show JavaScript still at the top.</li></ul>\n<h2>Code examples</h2>\n<p class=\"caption\"><span class=\"extra\">Extra:</span> Synchronous vs asynchronous in Node: the callback runs later, when the file read finishes</p><pre data-lang=\"js\">const fs = require(&quot;fs&quot;);\n\nconsole.log(&quot;1. start&quot;);\n\nfs.readFile(&quot;message.txt&quot;, &quot;utf8&quot;, (err, data) =&gt; {\n  // runs only when the 'file has been read' event fires\n  console.log(&quot;3. file says:&quot;, data);\n});\n\nconsole.log(&quot;2. this line does not wait for the file&quot;);</pre>\n<h2>Common mistakes</h2>\n<ul><li>Calling Node a framework or a language: it is a runtime that executes JavaScript outside the browser (Express, covered later, is the framework).</li><li>Assuming code always runs in the order written: with async APIs, the callback runs after the lines that follow it.</li></ul>");
PG.notes("l193", "<p class=\"summary\">Short article with the steps to install the LTS release of Node.js on macOS and check it worked from the terminal.</p>\n<h3>Install steps (macOS)</h3>\n<ul><li>Get the macOS installer from nodejs.org and pick the LTS (Long Term Support) build, the stable option shown on the left of the download page.</li><li>Double-click the downloaded .pkg to launch the installer.</li><li>On macOS Catalina (10.15) a security warning can block the .pkg. Workaround: Control-click (right-click) the file, choose Open With, then Installer (default).</li><li>Accept the licence, keep clicking Next, and leave the default install options.</li><li>Verify in the terminal (the course uses Hyper) with node --version; it should print the version number you just installed.</li></ul>\n<h3><span class=\"extra\">Extra:</span> Useful checks</h3>\n<ul><li><span class=\"extra\">Extra:</span> The installer also adds npm, the package manager used later in this section; npm --version confirms it.</li><li><span class=\"extra\">Extra:</span> If the command is not found, close and reopen the terminal so it picks up the updated PATH.</li></ul>\n<h2>Code examples</h2>\n<p class=\"caption\">Confirm the install</p><pre data-lang=\"sh\">node --version   # e.g. v20.x.x\nnpm --version    # (Extra) npm ships with Node</pre>\n<h2>Common mistakes</h2>\n<ul><li>Downloading the 'Current' build instead of LTS.</li><li>Catalina blocking the .pkg: open it via Control-click &gt; Open With &gt; Installer.</li></ul>");
PG.notes("l194", "<p class=\"summary\">Short article with the steps to install the LTS release of Node.js on Windows, including the required restart, and to confirm the install in Hyper.</p>\n<h3>Install steps (Windows)</h3>\n<ul><li>Get the Windows installer from nodejs.org and choose the LTS build (left-hand option on the download page).</li><li>Run the downloaded .msi installer.</li><li>Accept the licence, click Next through the screens and keep the default settings.</li><li>Restart the PC afterwards. Node commands will not work in the terminal until after a reboot.</li><li>Open Hyper and run node --version to confirm; the installed version number should appear.</li></ul>\n<h3><span class=\"extra\">Extra:</span> Useful checks</h3>\n<ul><li><span class=\"extra\">Extra:</span> Keep the installer's default option that adds Node to PATH; without it the node command is not recognised.</li><li><span class=\"extra\">Extra:</span> npm comes bundled with Node; npm --version confirms it is ready for the NPM lessons later in the section.</li></ul>\n<h2>Code examples</h2>\n<p class=\"caption\">Confirm the install (in Hyper / Git Bash)</p><pre data-lang=\"sh\">node --version   # e.g. v20.x.x\nnpm --version    # (Extra)</pre>\n<h2>Common mistakes</h2>\n<ul><li>Skipping the restart, then getting 'node is not recognized' in the terminal.</li><li>Picking the Current release instead of LTS.</li></ul>");
PG.notes("l195", "<p class=\"summary\">Checks the installed Node version from the VS Code terminal, introduces the Node REPL (enter, try JS, exit), and then runs a whole JavaScript file with the node command from the correct folder.</p>\n<h3>Version check in the VS Code terminal</h3>\n<ul><li>Open a new VS Code window, then View &gt; Terminal (or drag the panel up from the bottom edge of the window).</li><li>node -v (same as node --version) prints the installed version, starting with a 'v'. The course needs version 18 or later.</li><li>If your number differs: a higher one is fine (newer release); a lower one usually means an older install is still on the machine. The previous install lesson's notes/FAQ explain the fix.</li></ul>\n<h3>The Node REPL</h3>\n<ul><li>REPL = Read, Eval, Print, Loop: an interactive prompt that reads a line of code, evaluates it, prints the result, then waits for the next line. Most languages have one; it is not Node-specific.</li><li>Start it by typing node on its own. A welcome message and a &gt; prompt show you are inside.</li><li>.help lists the REPL's dot-commands, e.g. .break, .clear, .editor, .exit.</li><li>.exit leaves the REPL (the &gt; prompt disappears). Pressing Ctrl+C twice also exits.</li><li>Ctrl+C is the general escape hatch for the command line: if a process is running and you have lost your normal prompt, press it once or twice.</li><li>Any JavaScript works because Node is a JavaScript runtime: 5 + 8 prints 13; let a = 3 then a + 12 prints 15.</li><li>It behaves like the browser's DevTools console, but runs outside the browser.</li></ul>\n<h3>Running a JavaScript file with Node</h3>\n<ul><li>The REPL is handy for quick tests, but real apps are written in files that Node executes.</li><li>Starting files: download the lesson zip '2.1 Using Node' from the lecture resources, extract it and open the folder in VS Code. It holds an empty index.js.</li><li>Write code in index.js and save it.</li><li>In the terminal, cd into the folder that contains the file. Dragging the folder into the terminal after typing cd fills in its path.</li><li>Run node followed by the file name: node index.js. Node loads the file, executes it top to bottom, and console.log output appears in the terminal.</li><li>Tab completion: type the first letters of a file name and press Tab to auto-complete it. If nothing completes, the file isn't in the current directory, which is a quick check that you are in the right folder.</li></ul>\n<h2>Code examples</h2>\n<p class=\"caption\">Terminal session: version check and REPL</p><pre data-lang=\"sh\">node -v\n# v20.11.0  (any version &gt;= 18)\n\nnode\n# Welcome to Node.js ...\n&gt; 5 + 8\n13\n&gt; let a = 3\nundefined\n&gt; a + 12\n15\n&gt; .help\n&gt; .exit</pre>\n<p class=\"caption\">index.js and how to run it</p><pre data-lang=\"js\">// index.js\nconsole.log(&quot;Hello from Node&quot;);\n\n// in the terminal, from the folder that contains index.js:\n//   cd &quot;2.1 Using Node&quot;\n//   node index.js\n//   -&gt; Hello from Node</pre>\n<h2>The task: 2.1 Using Node: run your first file</h2>\n<div class=\"task-box\">\n<p><b>Starting point:</b> Extracted '2.1 Using Node' folder opened in VS Code, containing a blank index.js.</p>\n<p><b>Goal:</b> Execute a JavaScript file with the Node runtime from the terminal and see its output.</p>\n<p><b>Steps</b></p><ol><li>Add console.log(&quot;Hello from Node&quot;); to index.js and save.</li><li>Open the integrated terminal (View &gt; Terminal).</li><li>cd into the '2.1 Using Node' folder (type cd, then drag the folder in, press Enter).</li><li>Type node ind and press Tab so it completes to node index.js; press Enter.</li><li>Extra practice suggested: create a second .js file in the same folder, write some code and run it with node &lt;filename&gt;.</li></ol>\n<p><b>Expected result:</b> The terminal prints Hello from Node and returns to the normal prompt.</p>\n</div>\n<p class=\"caption\">Solution</p><pre data-lang=\"js\">console.log(&quot;Hello from Node&quot;);</pre>\n<h2>Common mistakes</h2>\n<ul><li>Running node index.js from the wrong directory gives a 'Cannot find module' error; Tab not completing the name is the early warning.</li><li>Getting stuck in the REPL: use .exit or Ctrl+C twice.</li><li>Typing node with no file name opens the REPL instead of running your file.</li><li>An old Node version (&lt;18) left over from a previous install.</li></ul>");
PG.notes("l196", "<p class=\"summary\">Introduces Node's built-in (native) modules and uses the File System module, fs, to write a text file with fs.writeFile. The challenge is to read the file back with fs.readFile and print its contents, which requires passing a 'utf8' encoding to get text instead of a Buffer.</p>\n<h3>What native modules are</h3>\n<ul><li>Node ships with a set of modules that are available without installing anything: file system access (fs), networking (http, https), path handling (path), os, crypto and more. They are the reason Node can do things browser JavaScript cannot.</li><li>Comparable to the default apps bundled with an operating system: present out of the box, and you can add more later (npm packages, next lectures).</li><li>The Node API documentation lists every native module. Treat it as a reference you look things up in, not something to read start to finish.</li><li>Modules mentioned as coming up later in the course: http (HyperText Transfer Protocol) and path.</li></ul>\n<h3>Why file access is a Node-only feature</h3>\n<ul><li>JavaScript running in a browser is sandboxed: a website must never be able to read or write files on a visitor's machine.</li><li>Node runs JavaScript outside the browser, on your own computer or a server, so it is allowed to read and write files there. You write the code yourself, so the safety concern of untrusted websites does not apply.</li><li>On a deployed web app the files are written to the server's disk, not the user's.</li></ul>\n<h3>Loading a module with require (CommonJS)</h3>\n<ul><li>Two ways exist to pull a module into a file: CommonJS require() and ES module import. This lecture uses require; ESM import is covered in the next lecture.</li><li>Syntax: const fs = require(&quot;fs&quot;); The module name is a string. VS Code autocompletes the list of native module names once you type the opening quote.</li><li>fs is short for file system. The variable name is conventional; the methods are then reached with dot notation (fs.writeFile, fs.readFile).</li><li>The Node docs show examples in import form (import { writeFile } from 'node:fs'). The underlying method is the same.</li></ul>\n<h3>fs.writeFile(file, data, [options], callback)</h3>\n<ul><li>Argument 1, file: the name or path of the file to create. A bare name like &quot;message.txt&quot; is resolved relative to the folder you run node from, which here is the project folder containing index.js.</li><li>Argument 2, data: what to put in the file, here a string such as &quot;Hello from NodeJS!&quot;.</li><li>Optional options argument (e.g. encoding) can go between data and callback; the default encoding for strings is utf8.</li><li>Last argument, callback: a function Node calls when the write finishes. It receives err; if err is set, throw it, otherwise log a success message.</li><li>If the file already exists, writeFile replaces its contents completely.</li><li><span class=\"extra\">Extra:</span> writeFile is asynchronous: Node starts the write and keeps going, then runs the callback once the disk operation is done. That is why success is reported inside the callback rather than on the next line.</li></ul>\n<h3>Running the script</h3>\n<ul><li>Open the terminal, cd into the 2.2 Native Modules folder (your path depends on OS and where you keep projects), then run node index.js.</li><li>Output 'The file has been saved!' means no error occurred, and message.txt appears next to index.js in the VS Code explorer.</li></ul>\n<h3>fs.readFile(path, [options], callback) and encodings</h3>\n<ul><li>Argument 1: the path of the file to read, e.g. &quot;./message.txt&quot; (./ means the current folder).</li><li>Last argument: a callback receiving (err, data). data holds the file contents.</li><li>Without an encoding option, data is a raw Buffer, which console.log prints as hex bytes like &lt;Buffer 48 65 6c ...&gt;.</li><li>Pass &quot;utf8&quot; as the second argument (fs.readFile(&quot;./message.txt&quot;, &quot;utf8&quot;, callback)) to get a normal string back.</li><li>Comment out the writeFile call before testing readFile, otherwise running the script overwrites the text you edited in message.txt.</li></ul>\n<h3><span class=\"extra\">Extra:</span> Reference repo notes</h3>\n<ul><li><span class=\"extra\">Extra:</span> The reference repo's script.js/solution.js only shows the writeFile step (to file.txt, with a &quot;utf8&quot; options argument and console.error-based error handling). It does not contain the readFile challenge solution with message.txt, so use the solution in these notes.</li><li><span class=\"extra\">Extra:</span> script.js also has commented-out examples of fs.readFile, fs.unlink (delete a file) and fs.access with fs.constants.F_OK (check a file exists); see the code example below. Ignore the student's personal text in file.txt.</li></ul>\n<h2>Code examples</h2>\n<p class=\"caption\">Writing a file with the native fs module (CommonJS)</p><pre data-lang=\"js\">const fs = require(&quot;fs&quot;);\n\nfs.writeFile(&quot;message.txt&quot;, &quot;Hello from NodeJS!&quot;, (err) =&gt; {\n  if (err) throw err;\n  console.log(&quot;The file has been saved!&quot;);\n});</pre>\n<p class=\"caption\">Terminal: run from the folder that contains index.js</p><pre data-lang=\"sh\">cd &quot;path/to/2.2 Native Modules&quot;\nnode index.js\n# The file has been saved!</pre>\n<p class=\"caption\">Without an encoding, readFile returns a Buffer</p><pre data-lang=\"txt\">&lt;Buffer 48 65 6c 6c 6f 20 66 72 6f 6d ... &gt;</pre>\n<p class=\"caption\"><span class=\"extra\">Extra:</span> Other fs callback methods (adapted from the reference repo's commented examples)</p><pre data-lang=\"js\">const fs = require(&quot;fs&quot;);\n\n// Delete a file\nfs.unlink(&quot;message.txt&quot;, (err) =&gt; {\n  if (err) {\n    console.error(&quot;Error deleting the file:&quot;, err);\n    return;\n  }\n  console.log(&quot;File deleted&quot;);\n});\n\n// Check whether a file exists\nfs.access(&quot;message.txt&quot;, fs.constants.F_OK, (err) =&gt; {\n  console.log(err ? &quot;The file does not exist&quot; : &quot;The file exists&quot;);\n});\n\n// Note: both calls are asynchronous, so their order of completion is not guaranteed.</pre>\n<h2>The task: 2.2 Native Modules: read a file with fs.readFile</h2>\n<div class=\"task-box\">\n<p><b>Starting point:</b> Starter folder '2.2 Native Modules' with an empty index.js. During the lecture you add the writeFile code, which creates message.txt.</p>\n<p><b>Goal:</b> Edit the text inside message.txt, then write code that reads message.txt and logs its contents as readable text in the terminal.</p>\n<p><b>Steps</b></p><ol><li>Extract the starter zip and open the folder in VS Code.</li><li>In index.js add const fs = require(&quot;fs&quot;); and the fs.writeFile call; run node index.js once to create message.txt.</li><li>Open message.txt and change the text (e.g. a greeting with your own name), then save it.</li><li>Look up fs.readFile in the Node docs and copy its example pattern into index.js.</li><li>Call fs.readFile(&quot;./message.txt&quot;, &quot;utf8&quot;, (err, data) =&gt; { ... }) and inside the callback throw err if set, then console.log(data).</li><li>Comment out the writeFile block so it does not overwrite your edited text.</li><li>Run node index.js. If you see a &lt;Buffer ...&gt; output, add the &quot;utf8&quot; encoding argument and run again.</li><li>Compare with solution.js if something differs (spelling of readFile, path, missing encoding).</li></ol>\n<p><b>Expected result:</b> The terminal prints the exact text currently stored in message.txt, including your edits.</p>\n</div>\n<p class=\"caption\">Solution</p><pre data-lang=\"js\">const fs = require(&quot;fs&quot;);\n\n// fs.writeFile(&quot;message.txt&quot;, &quot;Hello from NodeJS!&quot;, (err) =&gt; {\n//   if (err) throw err;\n//   console.log(&quot;The file has been saved!&quot;);\n// });\n\nfs.readFile(&quot;./message.txt&quot;, &quot;utf8&quot;, (err, data) =&gt; {\n  if (err) throw err;\n  console.log(data);\n});</pre>\n<h2>Common mistakes</h2>\n<ul><li>Running node from the wrong folder: relative paths resolve against the current working directory, so the file is created or looked for in the wrong place (ENOENT error on read).</li><li>Forgetting the 'utf8' encoding on readFile and getting a Buffer instead of text.</li><li>Leaving writeFile active, which silently overwrites the file you edited before readFile runs.</li><li>Putting the callback in the wrong position: it must always be the last argument.</li><li>Not extracting the starter zip before opening it in VS Code.</li></ul>");
PG.notes("l197", "<p class=\"summary\">Short heads-up that the newest version of the superheroes npm package changed its API, so the code in the next lesson must use a named import instead of the default import.</p>\n<h3>API change in the superheroes package</h3>\n<ul><li>Installing the current release of superheroes gives a package that exposes a named export, randomSuperhero, rather than a default export object with a random method.</li><li>Old pattern: default-import the package, then call its random() method. New pattern: named-import randomSuperhero and call it directly.</li><li><span class=\"extra\">Extra:</span> This is the general ESM distinction: a default import takes whatever the module exports as default, while a named import in braces must match an exported name exactly. When a package updates, a lecture's import line can break even though the logic is the same.</li><li><span class=\"extra\">Extra:</span> Importing with the ES module syntax requires &quot;type&quot;: &quot;module&quot; in package.json (or a .mjs extension) when running under Node.</li></ul>\n<h2>Code examples</h2>\n<p class=\"caption\">Updated usage for the current superheroes package</p><pre data-lang=\"js\">import { randomSuperhero } from &quot;superheroes&quot;;\nconst name = randomSuperhero();\nconsole.log(name);</pre>\n<p class=\"caption\">Older usage the video shows (superheroes v3 default export; fails with v4)</p><pre data-lang=\"js\">import superheroes from &quot;superheroes&quot;;\nconst name = superheroes.random();</pre>\n<h2>Common mistakes</h2>\n<ul><li>Copying the default-import line shown in the next video fails with the latest package version: Node throws a SyntaxError saying the module does not provide an export named 'default'.</li><li>Mixing up default and named imports produces errors such as a missing export.</li></ul>");
PG.notes("l198", "<p class=\"summary\">Explains what npm is, how to initialise a project with npm init to get package.json, how to install and use a third-party package (sillyname), and how to switch a project to ES modules with &quot;type&quot;: &quot;module&quot;. The exercise is to install the superheroes package and print a random hero name.</p>\n<h3>What npm is</h3>\n<ul><li>npm (Node Package Manager) is a public, open-source registry of reusable modules written by other developers, run by GitHub. It is shared code you pull in instead of writing everything yourself.</li><li>Native modules are the built-in toolbox; npm packages are the community library of extra tools you add when a task needs one.</li><li>npm is installed together with Node, so no separate install is needed.</li><li>Packages are searched on npmjs.com. Each package page shows the install command and usage examples.</li></ul>\n<h3>Initialising a project with npm init</h3>\n<ul><li>cd into the project folder first, then run npm init. It starts an interactive wizard that produces package.json.</li><li>Prompts in order: package name (lecture uses learning-npm), version (default 1.0.0), description, entry point (default index.js), test command, git repository, keywords, author, license (default ISC). Pressing Enter keeps defaults/leaves blank.</li><li>At the end the wizard prints the JSON it will write and asks for confirmation; answer y.</li><li>package.json is a plain JSON file of key/value pairs describing the project. On its own it is only configuration; it becomes useful once packages are installed.</li><li><span class=\"extra\">Extra:</span> npm init -y accepts every default without prompting.</li></ul>\n<h3>Installing and using a package</h3>\n<ul><li>Install with npm install &lt;package-name&gt;. The shorthand npm i does the same. Several packages can be installed at once by listing names separated by spaces.</li><li>Installing adds a dependencies section to package.json recording the package name and version range, and creates a node_modules folder holding the downloaded package code (and its own dependencies).</li><li><span class=\"extra\">Extra:</span> Also generated: package-lock.json, which pins exact resolved versions. node_modules is normally excluded from git and can be rebuilt with npm install.</li><li>Usage steps: read the package's npm page, import it, call the function it exposes. For sillyname the exported function generates a random funny name.</li><li>The lecture first uses the older CommonJS pattern, var generateName = require(&quot;sillyname&quot;), then calls generateName() and logs the result in a template literal (backticks with ${} interpolation).</li><li>Run the file with node index.js to see a different silly name each time.</li></ul>\n<h3>CommonJS vs ES modules</h3>\n<ul><li>CJS (CommonJS) is the original Node system based on require(). ESM (ECMAScript modules) uses import/from and is the standard shared with front-end JavaScript. Node has supported ESM since about version 12.</li><li>To enable ESM in a project, add a top-level key in package.json: &quot;type&quot;: &quot;module&quot;. The default value is commonjs. The lecture places it just under &quot;main&quot;.</li><li>ESM syntax: import name from &quot;package&quot;. Typing the module path first (import {  } from &quot;pkg&quot;) lets VS Code autocomplete the exported names; typing the name first gives no suggestions.</li><li>Once import is used, the old require line can be deleted.</li></ul>\n<h3><span class=\"extra\">Extra:</span> Reference repo notes</h3>\n<ul><li><span class=\"extra\">Extra:</span> The reference script.js uses the current named import (import { randomSuperhero } from 'superheroes') with superheroes ^4.0.0 in package.json, while its solution.js still uses the old default import and superheroes.random(), which throws with v4.</li><li><span class=\"extra\">Extra:</span> Its solution.js imports &quot;sillyName&quot; with a capital N; the npm name is all lowercase, sillyname.</li></ul>\n<h2>Code examples</h2>\n<p class=\"caption\">Commands for the lecture</p><pre data-lang=\"sh\">cd path/to/project\nnpm init\nnpm i sillyname\nnode index.js</pre>\n<p class=\"caption\">package.json after init and install, with ESM enabled</p><pre data-lang=\"json\">{\n  &quot;name&quot;: &quot;learning-npm&quot;,\n  &quot;version&quot;: &quot;1.0.0&quot;,\n  &quot;description&quot;: &quot;I'm learning about npm&quot;,\n  &quot;main&quot;: &quot;index.js&quot;,\n  &quot;type&quot;: &quot;module&quot;,\n  &quot;scripts&quot;: {\n    &quot;test&quot;: &quot;echo \\&quot;Error: no test specified\\&quot; &amp;&amp; exit 1&quot;\n  },\n  &quot;license&quot;: &quot;ISC&quot;,\n  &quot;dependencies&quot;: {\n    &quot;sillyname&quot;: &quot;^0.1.0&quot;\n  }\n}</pre>\n<p class=\"caption\">sillyname with ES module import</p><pre data-lang=\"js\">import generateName from &quot;sillyname&quot;;\n\nvar sillyName = generateName();\nconsole.log(`My name is ${sillyName}.`);</pre>\n<h2>The task: Superhero name generator</h2>\n<div class=\"task-box\">\n<p><b>Starting point:</b> The same npm project and index.js used for sillyname, with package.json already set to &quot;type&quot;: &quot;module&quot;.</p>\n<p><b>Goal:</b> Use the superheroes package to print a random hero name in the console.</p>\n<p><b>Steps</b></p><ol><li>Find the superheroes package on npmjs.com (a link is in the course resources) and read its usage section.</li><li>In the project folder run npm i superheroes and confirm it appears under dependencies in package.json.</li><li>Comment out the sillyname code in index.js (or keep it; both can run in the same file).</li><li>Import the package at the top of index.js. With the current version this is a named import: import { randomSuperhero } from &quot;superheroes&quot;; (the package name string must match exactly).</li><li>Generate a name with const name = randomSuperhero(); (the video's older version used superheroes.random() on a default import).</li><li>console.log a sentence such as I am &lt;name&gt;! using a template literal: console.log(<code>I am ${name}!</code>).</li><li>Run node index.js; each run should print a different hero.</li></ol>\n<p><b>Expected result:</b> Console output like: I am Piledriver! (different each run).</p>\n</div>\n<p class=\"caption\">Solution</p><pre data-lang=\"js\">// index.js (package.json has &quot;type&quot;: &quot;module&quot;; superheroes 4.x installed)\nimport { randomSuperhero } from &quot;superheroes&quot;;\n\nconst name = randomSuperhero();\nconsole.log(`I am ${name}!`);\n\n// Older API shown in the video (superheroes v3 default export).\n// Does NOT work with v4, so keep it commented out:\n// import superheroes from &quot;superheroes&quot;;\n// const name = superheroes.random();\n// console.log(`I am ${name}!`);</pre>\n<h2>Common mistakes</h2>\n<ul><li>SyntaxError: Cannot use import statement outside a module means &quot;type&quot;: &quot;module&quot; is missing from package.json.</li><li>The package name in the import string must match the npm name exactly, including case (the reference solution has an import of &quot;sillyName&quot; with a capital N that would fail on case-sensitive systems).</li><li>The latest superheroes version uses a named export, so superheroes.random() fails; use randomSuperhero().</li><li>Run npm commands from the project folder or package.json lands in the wrong place.</li></ul>");
PG.notes("l199", "<p class=\"summary\">Capstone for the Node section: a command-line program that asks for a URL with the inquirer package, turns it into a PNG QR code with qr-image, and saves the URL to a text file with the native fs module. The walkthrough doubles as practice at reading package documentation and converting CommonJS examples to ES module imports.</p>\n<h3>Project overview and packages</h3>\n<ul><li>A QR code is a 2D barcode that a phone camera scans to open a URL.</li><li>inquirer collects typed input from the terminal. qr-image generates QR images (PNG by default). fs (native) writes files.</li><li>Spelling matters: inquirer is easy to misspell, and qr-image has a hyphen, not an underscore. Verify names on npmjs.com before installing.</li><li>Install both in one command: npm i inquirer qr-image. The install reported about 50 packages because each package brings its own dependencies into node_modules; they are small.</li><li><span class=\"extra\">Extra:</span> fs is a built-in module and needs no install; the reference repo's package.json lists an &quot;fs&quot; security placeholder package, which is unnecessary.</li></ul>\n<h3>Preparing package.json for ES modules</h3>\n<ul><li>The project had no full package.json, which shows npm init -y can be run any time, even after installing packages; -y accepts all defaults.</li><li>Add &quot;type&quot;: &quot;module&quot; so import syntax works. Without it Node reports that import cannot be used outside a module.</li><li><span class=\"extra\">Extra:</span> Optionally fill in the author field.</li></ul>\n<h3>Step 1: get user input with inquirer</h3>\n<ul><li>The docs example calls inquirer.prompt([...questions]) which returns a promise. .then receives an answers object; .catch handles errors.</li><li>Each question is an object. message is the text shown to the user; name is the key under which the answer is stored. Many other options exist and are optional. Several questions can be passed in the array.</li><li>Question used: { message: &quot;Type in your URL&quot;, name: &quot;URL&quot; }.</li><li>First log answers to see its shape: an object such as { URL: &quot;www.google.com&quot; }. Then read answers.URL into a const url.</li><li>Run from the project folder with node index.js. (Extra) The catch block's error.isTtyError check distinguishes a terminal that cannot show prompts from other failures.</li></ul>\n<h3>Step 2: create the QR image with qr-image</h3>\n<ul><li>The docs example uses require. It must be converted: import qr from &quot;qr-image&quot; and import fs from &quot;fs&quot;. CommonJS require and ESM import cannot be mixed in one file.</li><li>qr.image(text, options) returns a readable stream. The options object (type, size, margin and so on) is optional; the default type is PNG, so it can be omitted.</li><li>Pipe the stream into a file: qr_svg.pipe(fs.createWriteStream(&quot;qr_img.png&quot;)). This writes the PNG into the project folder.</li><li>Test with a full URL including https:// and scan the result with a phone to confirm it opens the right site.</li><li><span class=\"extra\">Extra:</span> The qr-image README example passes { type: 'svg' } and names its variable qr_svg; the lecture drops the type option so the output is PNG (the default), and the variable name has no effect.</li></ul>\n<h3>Step 3: save the URL with fs.writeFile</h3>\n<ul><li>Call fs.writeFile(&quot;URL.txt&quot;, url, callback) inside the same .then block, after the image code.</li><li>The callback receives err; throw it if present, otherwise log a confirmation message.</li><li>Final outcome of one run: a qr_img.png and a URL.txt containing the typed text in the project folder.</li></ul>\n<h3>Workflow lessons</h3>\n<ul><li>Read each package's documentation first, paste its example, then adapt it. The qr-image source lives in node_modules/qr-image/lib, which can be browsed to see what is exported.</li><li>Typing the import line before using a name lets the editor autocomplete.</li><li>Getting stuck and digging through docs is the expected part of the process. Next section: Express.</li></ul>\n<h2>Code examples</h2>\n<p class=\"caption\">Finished index.js (reconstructed from the reference solution)</p><pre data-lang=\"js\">import inquirer from &quot;inquirer&quot;;\nimport qr from &quot;qr-image&quot;;\nimport fs from &quot;fs&quot;;\n\ninquirer\n  .prompt([\n    {\n      message: &quot;Type in your URL: &quot;,\n      name: &quot;URL&quot;,\n    },\n  ])\n  .then((answers) =&gt; {\n    const url = answers.URL;\n\n    // QR image: PNG by default\n    const qr_png = qr.image(url);\n    qr_png.pipe(fs.createWriteStream(&quot;qr_img.png&quot;));\n\n    // Save the typed URL\n    fs.writeFile(&quot;URL.txt&quot;, url, (err) =&gt; {\n      if (err) throw err;\n      console.log(&quot;The file has been saved!&quot;);\n    });\n  })\n  .catch((error) =&gt; {\n    if (error.isTtyError) {\n      // Prompt could not be rendered in this environment\n    } else {\n      // Something else went wrong\n    }\n  });</pre>\n<p class=\"caption\">Setup commands</p><pre data-lang=\"sh\">npm init -y\nnpm i inquirer qr-image\nnode index.js</pre>\n<h2>The task: QR Code Generator</h2>\n<div class=\"task-box\">\n<p><b>Starting point:</b> index.js with a comment block listing three TODOs, an empty or partial package.json.</p>\n<p><b>Goal:</b> Running node index.js asks for a URL, writes a QR code PNG for it and a text file containing the URL.</p>\n<p><b>Steps</b></p><ol><li>Install inquirer and qr-image with npm i (check spelling on npmjs.com).</li><li>Make sure package.json has &quot;type&quot;: &quot;module&quot;.</li><li>Import inquirer and call inquirer.prompt with one question object that has message and name.</li><li>In .then, read answers.URL into a const.</li><li>Import qr from qr-image; create the image with qr.image(url) and pipe it to fs.createWriteStream(&quot;qr_img.png&quot;).</li><li>Import fs and call fs.writeFile to save the URL to URL.txt, with an error-throwing callback.</li><li>Run it, enter a full https URL, then scan the PNG with a phone to check it.</li></ol>\n<p><b>Expected result:</b> After entering e.g. https://www.google.com, the folder contains qr_img.png (scans to that URL) and URL.txt holding the same text.</p>\n</div>\n<p class=\"caption\">Solution</p><pre data-lang=\"js\">import inquirer from &quot;inquirer&quot;;\nimport qr from &quot;qr-image&quot;;\nimport fs from &quot;fs&quot;;\n\ninquirer\n  .prompt([{ message: &quot;Type in your URL: &quot;, name: &quot;URL&quot; }])\n  .then((answers) =&gt; {\n    const url = answers.URL;\n    // qr.image returns a readable PNG stream; pipe it into a file\n    qr.image(url).pipe(fs.createWriteStream(&quot;qr_img.png&quot;));\n    // save the typed URL as plain text\n    fs.writeFile(&quot;URL.txt&quot;, url, (err) =&gt; {\n      if (err) throw err;\n      console.log(&quot;The file has been saved!&quot;);\n    });\n  })\n  .catch((error) =&gt; {\n    if (error.isTtyError) {\n      console.error(&quot;Prompt could not be rendered in this terminal&quot;);\n    } else {\n      console.error(error);\n    }\n  });</pre>\n<h2>Common mistakes</h2>\n<ul><li>Misspelling the package names (inquirer, qr-image with a hyphen).</li><li>Forgetting &quot;type&quot;: &quot;module&quot;, which causes the import error.</li><li>Leaving require() lines from the docs alongside import statements.</li><li>Typing a URL without https:// makes a QR code phones may treat as plain text.</li><li>Running node from the wrong folder so files land elsewhere.</li><li>The reference repo's index.js differs slightly from the lecture: it saves to link.txt rather than URL.txt and logs placeholder 'Error 1/2' messages; its package.json also lists an unnecessary &quot;fs&quot; placeholder dependency and the student's name as author (ignore both).</li></ul>");
PG.notes("l200", "<p class=\"summary\">A short motivational tip encouraging learners to treat self-doubt as a signal to prepare and take on bigger challenges.</p>\n<h3>Key takeaway</h3>\n<ul><li>Self-doubt when handed a demanding opportunity is common; preparing thoroughly and accepting the task is how skills grow.</li><li>Watch for moments of doubt paired with a request to step up, and treat them as growth opportunities rather than reasons to decline.</li></ul>");
